  "body": "The maintainer asked for this research on 2026-11-18. It is the second GPU research issue, after #2525 (nesting).\n\nQuestion: can a GPU shader generate or help to generate the toolpaths of operations?\n\nOpen questions for the research:\n- Candidates: find which operation steps map to the GPU. Examples: a height field of the model (drop-cutter, tool-offset field), 2D offset by distance field (pocket, contour, adaptive clearing), rest-material and stock removal fields, collision and gouge checks over a grid.\n- Accuracy: a grid has a resolution limit. What resolution holds the tolerance of an operation (for example 0.01 mm over a 500 mm part)? Can an exact check with the vector or mesh geometry remove each risk? A toolpath that gouges is the worst failure (Prime Directive).\n- Speed: compare with the CPU generators of `camcraft-motion` and `camcraft-cl`. Estimate the gain for each candidate on the large fixtures. Include the cost of the transfer between CPU and GPU.\n- Platform: the project builds on Windows, Linux and macOS, and in the browser build (wgpu). Which compute path works on all of them? Issues #2424 and #74 show that GPU drivers fail on some machines. What happens with no GPU or a bad adapter (CPU fallback, refusal with a diagnostic)?\n- Determinism: the same input must give the same toolpath and the same G-code on each machine. Do GPU results differ between drivers (float rules)? How do we pin this with golden files in `tests/golden/`?\n- Architecture: where does the code live (a new crate, or `camcraft-motion`)? Which dependency would it add? A new `camcraft-*` dependency needs a decision entry. How does the work fit the command model and the stock simulation?\n- Relation to the existing simulation: the stock simulation may already use the GPU. Find what exists, and what can be shared.\n\nDone means: comments on this issue answer each question above, with sources. The research ends with a recommendation (use, do not use, or use only as a preview or first filter before an exact CPU result) and the follow-up issues, each with one `P*` and one `effort:*` label. Then close this issue.\n\nUse only published literature and general knowledge (Clean-Room Protocol).\n",
